The '''History of Sarta (1705–1747)''' covers the period from the [[Treaty of Bahia Alta]] to the end of the [[Sartan Civil War]] in 1747. It was marked by the loss of direct sea access, the creation of a constitutional monarchy under a royal parliamentary court, repeated internal unrest, and renewed foreign intervention. The kingdom remained formally intact, but its political authority was increasingly divided between the crown, the baronage, and parliamentary institutions.

== After the Treaty of Bahia Alta ==

The [[Treaty of Bahia Alta]] ended the [[Sarta–Volisania War]] and left Sarta without direct access to the sea. The kingdom retained limited river transit rights through Volisanian territory, but its coastal position was weakened and its foreign trade was constrained. The loss of the coast forced the crown to rely more heavily on inland administration, landed wealth, and negotiated access to external markets.

In the years after the treaty, the crown and its supporters began selling lands at home and abroad to nobles and wealthy families in order to stabilise royal finances and reward loyalty. Some of the wealthiest Sartans moved to Guajicutea or to Volisanian-controlled Sartan territory, where coastal trade and security remained stronger. The policy increased the power of the elite, but it also deepened resentment among poorer rural communities that remained tied to feudal farms. At the same time, [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] drew closer to Sarta, in part because it feared further Volisanian expansion.

== Constitutional monarchy and the royal parliamentary court ==

Political change in the early eighteenth century took place under the influence of liberalismo in other Illypnian states, but the resulting settlement remained highly oligarchic. The crown retained executive authority, and the parliament was preserved rather than abolished, yet the state continued to be dominated by nobles, barons, and court factions. The new order was constitutional in form, but it did not significantly redistribute wealth or broaden political participation.

In 1716, after [[Carlos I of Sarta]] was forced to abdicate, a royal parliamentary court was established. The court was intended to balance the crown with parliamentary authority and to give the baron class a formal role in government. Maria I of Sarta and her house relied on the support of the barons, who expected influence in return for backing the settlement. The new arrangement preserved the monarchy while making it more dependent on elite cooperation.

The system remained unstable. Many elites considered the court too close to Volisania, while others hoped it would protect their own estates and privileges. The majority of the population, meanwhile, saw little change in daily life. Rights remained limited, movement was restricted in many districts, and the rural population continued to work on feudal farms with little access to the new political order.

== The move to Leona ==

As the new constitutional order took shape, the capital was moved south to [[Leona]], a central inland city. The relocation was intended to place the court in a more neutral position between the northern highlands and the coastal regions, and to reduce dependence on the weakened coastal zone. Leona became the main seat of the royal parliamentary court and the centre of the kingdom's political life.

The move did not end regional tension. Royalists in the north resented the loss of influence, while parliamentary supporters and coastal interests used Leona to consolidate their position. Internal squabbling continued at court, and the government increasingly relied on restrictions on assembly, travel, and movement to manage unrest.

== War of the Isthmus ==

The [[War of the Isthmus]] began in 1711 and continued until 1716. The conflict gave Sarta a modest degree of relief from Volisanian pressure, since Volisania was forced to direct attention and resources elsewhere. Although the war did not restore Sarta's former coastal position, it created room for the new constitutional settlement to take shape.

The war also weakened the legitimacy of the old absolutist model. By the time it ended, the king had already abdicated, and the royal parliamentary court had become the main framework for governance. The settlement that followed was a compromise between liberal constitutional influence, elite bargaining, and the need to prevent further state collapse.

== Social strain and unrest ==

The new political order did little to relieve social inequality. Wealth remained concentrated among nobles, merchants, and large landholders, while the rural population stayed on feudal farms. The failure of the state to spread wealth more widely fed resentment across the kingdom.

Peasant revolts flared repeatedly in the countryside, and the government responded with restrictions on rights, movement, and local assembly. The political elite remained divided, with some factions loyal to the north and others favouring the royal parliamentary court. The court itself became a site of constant internal dispute, as aristocratic groups competed for access to patronage, offices, and land.

The fact that the richest parts of the former Sartan coast remained under Volisanian control intensified resentment further. Many nobles and royalists believed those territories would eventually be recovered, while others treated the loss as a humiliation that had to be endured until the balance of power changed.

== Maria I of Sarta ==

Maria I of Sarta emerged as the dominant figure in Sartan politics during this unstable period. Her rise was supported by parliament and by those elites who saw her as a practical answer to the kingdom's postwar weakness. At the same time, she was viewed with suspicion by many northern royalists, who associated her with Volisanian backing and with the continued loss of Sartan coastal wealth.

Maria retained the parliamentary settlement, but she governed in an increasingly hard-edged way. The constitutional monarchy remained intact, yet the state became more absolutist in practice. Rural unrest continued, and the government relied on factional alliances, legal restrictions, and elite bargains to keep control. Maria's position was never secure enough to eliminate opposition, and her rule was shaped by the tension between compromise and coercion.

In this period, the capital was moved again, this time to [[Mariapolis]] on [[Lake Divisora]]. The new city lay on the coast of the lake, north of the Sierra Madre and near the centre of the kingdom. The move reflected both the desire to distance the court from the north-south factional split and the need to create a new political centre outside the older rival capitals.

== The First Sartan Civil War ==

The [[First Sartan Civil War]] began in 1738 and lasted until 1747. It was one of the most destructive conflicts in Sartan history, killing roughly 10% of the population. The war grew out of the unresolved tensions of the previous decades: baronial rivalry, popular unrest, resentment over Volisanian influence, and disputes over the authority of Maria's regime.

Volisania and royalist factions backed Maria I of Sarta, who became the central claimant around which the conflict was fought. The war devastated the countryside and further strained the urban economy. The capital at Mariapolis became the new focus of government as the conflict unfolded, but the violence spread across much of the kingdom.

The war ended in 1747 with Maria I of Sarta in control of the kingdom, but the settlement remained fragile and heavily dependent on elite compromise.

== Aftermath ==

By the end of the period, Sarta had become a more divided and fragile kingdom than it had been in 1705. The loss of direct sea access, the rise of the parliamentary court, the move to Leona, the establishment of Mariapolis, and the civil war had transformed the state. The crown survived, but it did so through compromise and repeated internal conflict.

The period left behind a political order that was constitutional in form, oligarchic in practice, and unstable in its social foundations. It also set the stage for the next phase of Sartan history, in which the kingdom would continue to struggle over the balance between royal authority, parliamentary power, and regional faction.

